<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/1st-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Revolt</p><h1 class="enc-title">Boudican revolt</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">60–61</span> · <a href="/centuries/1st-century">1st century</a> · Also known as Boudica's revolt, Revolt of Boudica</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Boudican revolt was an uprising of the Iceni and neighbouring peoples of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> against Roman rule in 60 or 61 CE, led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boudica">Boudica</a>, widow of the Iceni king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prasutagus">Prasutagus</a>. Provoked by the seizure of the Iceni kingdom and the abuse of Boudica and her daughters by Roman officials, the rebels destroyed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/camulodunum">Camulodunum</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/londinium">Londinium</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/verulamium">Verulamium</a>, killing, according to Roman writers, some 70,000 to 80,000 people. The governor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaius-suetonius-paulinus">Gaius Suetonius Paulinus</a> defeated them in a decisive battle, after which Roman rule in the province was firmly re-established.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> invaded south-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> in 43 CE and conquered it gradually, leaving some kingdoms nominally independent as allies. The Iceni king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prasutagus">Prasutagus</a> tried to secure his kingdom by leaving it jointly to his daughters and the emperor, but on his death his will was ignored. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tacitus">Tacitus</a> describes the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> seizing land and enslaving Iceni; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boudica">Boudica</a> was publicly flogged and her daughters raped. According to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cassius-dio">Cassius Dio</a>, Roman financiers such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seneca-the-younger">Seneca the Younger</a> also called in their loans. The only surviving accounts of the revolt are those of Tacitus and Dio.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>While the governor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaius-suetonius-paulinus">Gaius Suetonius Paulinus</a> was campaigning against the island of Mona, a stronghold of the druids, the Iceni rose with the Trinovantes and others. They first attacked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/camulodunum">Camulodunum</a>, a colonia of Roman veterans with a costly temple to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/claudius">Claudius</a>, routed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/legio-ix-hispana">Legio IX Hispana</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quintus-petillius-cerialis">Quintus Petillius Cerialis</a>, and after a two-day siege of the temple destroyed the city. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suetonius">Suetonius</a> reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/londinium">Londinium</a> but judged his troops too few and abandoned it, taking with him those who would leave; the rebels burned it and killed those who stayed, and then destroyed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/verulamium">Verulamium</a>. Archaeologists find a layer of burnt debris, the Boudican Destruction Horizon, in all three towns.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suetonius">Suetonius</a> gathered almost 10,000 men, including the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/legio-xiv-gemina">Legio XIV Gemina</a>, and took a stand in a narrow passage with a wood behind him, at a place that has never been identified. Ancient writers put the Britons at 230,000 to 300,000, figures modern historians treat with scepticism. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/legion">legionaries</a> advanced in a wedge, the flight of the Britons was blocked by their own wagons, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> spared neither women nor animals. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tacitus">Tacitus</a> says <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boudica">Boudica</a> then poisoned herself; Dio says she fell ill and died.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>Suetonius Tranquillus writes that the crisis almost persuaded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nero">Nero</a> to abandon <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>. Poenius <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/postumus">Postumus</a>, whose <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/legio-ii-augusta">Legio II Augusta</a> had not answered the summons, fell on his sword. Suetonius carried out widespread punitive operations, and after criticism from the new procurator <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/julius-classicianus">Gaius Julius Alpinus Classicianus</a> and an inquiry by the freedman Polyclitus, Nero replaced him with the more conciliatory Publius Petronius Turpilianus. The defeat ended resistance to Roman rule in most of southern Britain, though the north remained unsettled.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Boudican revolt” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
